  "conclusions": [
    "In both original SFT prompt templates, adding the behavior-preserving no-op replacement switches the first token from66582 to788 for both harmful and redundant guards: four flips in four matched comparisons.",
    "Retaining an unchanged identical guard changes the correct verdict from concern to clean while keeping the first token unchanged in all four matched comparisons. The observed first-token route follows the prescribed diff-shape manipulation across these eight prompts.",
    "Both original harmful-deletion cases reproduce every generated token and complete response byte-for-byte. This directly connects the matched controls to two of the original six deletion-associated training wins.",
    "All four replacement responses copy changed lines instead of explaining a mechanism. Both NULL deletion responses invent a scope defect. The permission harmful-deletion response contains the relevant permission requirement alongside a false scope claim; the redundant permission deletion invents an AGENTS.md runtime effect.",
    "The expanded-context audit and matched-template control occupy different input conditions: the expanded audit selected788 for all eight prompts, while preserving the original SFT template exposes the exact shape-sensitive pattern. Together they identify sensitivity to both the matched shape manipulation and the wider prompt-content changes.",
    "The matched shape manipulation includes the no-op added line, its required hunk count and its required changed-line ID2. This experiment measures their combined effect. Its observed scope is the selected control checkpoint, shared-prefix route and these two original templates.",
    "No generated answer is a complete correct review under the predeclared semantic rubric. Two responses pass the production citation parser, both permission pure deletions; parser acceptance and first-token classification are recorded separately from semantic correctness."
  ],
